### Phase 5: Dockerization & Deployment

I am now containerizing the "Premium SaaS" application to ensure it is portable and production-ready. This process involves setting up a custom Nginx server for SPA routing and a multi-stage Docker build.

#### 1. Creating the Nginx Configuration
This configuration ensures that the SPA handles routing correctly and that static assets are served efficiently.

```nginx
# apps/premium-saas/nginx.conf
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name localhost;

    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index index.html;

    # Gzip Compression
    gzip on;
    gzip_vary on;
    gzip_min_length 10240;
    gzip_proxied expired no-cache no-store private auth;
    gzip_types text/plain text/css text/xml text/javascript application/x-javascript application/xml;
    gzip_disable "MSIE [1-6]\.";

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }

    # Cache static assets
    location ~* \.(js|css|png|jpg|jpeg|gif|ico|svg)$ {
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, no-transform";
    }

    # Security headers
    add_header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN";
    add_header X-XSS-Protection "1; mode=block";
    add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff";
}
```

#### 2. Creating the Dockerfile
I am implementing a multi-stage build. This separates the build environment (Node.js) from the runtime environment (Nginx), resulting in a significantly smaller and more secure production image.

```dockerfile
# apps/premium-saas/Dockerfile

# Stage 1: Build
FROM node:20-alpine AS build

WORKDIR /app

# Copy package files first for better layer caching
COPY package*.json ./
RUN npm install

# Copy source code and build
COPY . .
RUN npm run build

# Stage 2: Production
FROM nginx:stable-alpine

# Copy custom nginx config
COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf

# Copy build output from stage 1
COPY --from=build /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html

EXPOSE 80

CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]
```

#### 3. Building and Running the Container
I will now execute the build process and launch the container on the specified port.

